<Project>
  <!-- Overrides for building the tests standalone, outside the Rhino source tree. The in-Rhino
       build ('$(InRhino)' == 'True') is deliberately untouched throughout this file. -->

  <!-- 1. Target framework.

       Both target frameworks run standalone: net48 through the <codeBase> bindings written below,
       net10.0-windows through the assembly resolvers Rhino.Testing installs when it boots Rhino.
       So the csproj's own net48;net10.0-windows stands, and this file no longer narrows it.

       The escape hatch is opt-in: build or test with -p:MxStandaloneNet48Only=true to drop
       net10.0-windows, which is worth having if a Rhino WIP update breaks the net10 test host
       again. It must live in a .targets file, not .props: TargetFrameworks is assigned in the
       csproj body, and .targets is imported after it. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(InRhino)' != 'True' AND '$(MxStandaloneNet48Only)' == 'true'">
    <TargetFrameworks>net48</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- 2. net48 reference conflicts, MSB3277 x2.

       On net48 the test platform pulls both halves of a split graph: Microsoft.TestPlatform.-
       ObjectModel 17.12.0 asks for System.Reflection.Metadata 1.6.0, which in turn asks for
       System.Collections.Immutable 1.5.0 (assembly versions 1.4.2.0 and 1.2.3.0), while
       Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.VSTestBridge is compiled against 8.0.0.0 of both. Neither is a
       direct reference, so RAR picks the 1.x pair for being primary and warns that it could not
       resolve the conflict - once per assembly, in every project.

       Referencing the 8.0.0 packages directly makes 8.0.0.0 the primary and the exact version
       VSTestBridge wants, and lets the 1.x chain unify upwards. netcoreapp resolves this on its
       own, hence net48 only. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(InRhino)' != 'True' AND '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net48'">
    <PackageReference Include="System.Collections.Immutable" Version="8.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Reflection.Metadata" Version="8.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- 3. Assembly binding, net48 only.

       Standalone, there is no RhinoCommon.dll next to the test assembly - the csproj references
       the package with ExcludeAssets="runtime" on purpose, because the managed RhinoCommon must
       load out of Rhino's own System folder or its native core (RhinoLibrary.dll) is not found.
       With nothing bound, NUnit cannot load the test assembly and discovers zero tests; with a
       copy in the output folder instead, discovery works but the test host crashes on run.

       So bind RhinoCommon / Rhino.UI / Eto straight to the install with <codeBase>. The versions
       are read from the installed files on every build rather than hardcoded, because the
       floating RhinoCommon package version and the Rhino WIP install drift apart independently -
       a fixed version here goes stale at the next WIP update and the tests vanish again.

       net10.0-windows needs no counterpart: <codeBase> is a .NET Framework mechanism, and there
       Rhino.Testing resolves RhinoCommon out of RhinoSystemDirectory itself. That resolver is
       installed when Rhino boots, which is after NUnit has scanned the test assembly, so a test
       assembly must not force RhinoCommon to load during that scan - see the note on
       StepMetrics.Bbox in FileIO\STEP\StepImportBase.cs.

       Runs after the SDK's own GenerateBindingRedirects, so it amends that output. -->
  <Target Name="MxWriteRhinoBindings"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(InRhino)' != 'True'
                     AND '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net48'
                     AND Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\Rhino.Testing.Configs.xml')
                     AND Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build\write-rhino-bindings.ps1')">
    <XmlPeek XmlInputPath="$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\Rhino.Testing.Configs.xml"
             Query="/Settings/RhinoSystemDirectory/text()">
      <Output TaskParameter="Result" PropertyName="MxRhinoSystemDir" />
    </XmlPeek>

    <!-- The trailing backslash of "...\System\" would escape the closing quote of the command
         line, so hand the script a trimmed path. -->
    <Exec Condition="'$(MxRhinoSystemDir)' != ''"
          Command="powershell -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build\write-rhino-bindings.ps1&quot; -ConfigPath &quot;$(OutputPath)$(TargetFileName).config&quot; -RhinoSystemDir &quot;$(MxRhinoSystemDir.TrimEnd('\'))&quot;"
          StandardOutputImportance="high" />
  </Target>
</Project>
